  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This study mapped land use and land cover changes in Jammu City across 2002, 2014, and 2024 using Landsat imagery and a supervised Maximum Likelihood Classification model. The classification achieved overall accuracies between 94.37% and 96.12%, revealing that built-up areas expanded by 75.1% (from 3,326 ha to 5,825 ha) while dense vegetation shrank from 9.4% to 5.1% of the study area. Agricultural land showed a non-linear trend, expanding to 54.3% of the territory in 2014 before dropping to 35.8% in 2024.",
      "why": "It establishes a detailed, 22-year empirical baseline of urban sprawl and vegetation loss in a rapidly growing Indian city where long-term spatial transformation trends were previously undocumented."
    }
